  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Elevation dependent wet snowfall in the Northern\nTier and Endless Mountains continues into the morning.\n\nLight precipitation on the western edge of low pressure moving\noff to our east has changed over to snow for the higher\nelevations of northeast PA. Road temperatures are largely \nexpected to remain above freezing, so any accumulations should \nbe limited to unpaved surfaces. The Winter Weather Advisory for\nPotter, Tioga, Northern Lycoming, and Sullivan Counties remains\nin effect through 1 PM today as some parts of those zones could\nsee an additional 1 to 2 inches of snow before precipitation \ntapers off this afternoon. Areas farther to the south could see\nsome snow mix in with rain this morning, but impacts will be \nminimal.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Breezy and colder weekend with rain and snow \nshowers downwind of Lake Erie. \n\nWinds initially out of the north-northeast become more north-\nnorthwesterly later today, helping to direct some moisture from\nthe Great Lakes into northern and western PA. Though the fetch\nacross Lake Erie will be relatively short, model RH profiles \nsuggest that the moisture should be deep enough to support some \nlake effect rain and snow showers across the northwest \nmountains. The wind direction should also become more favorable \nfor upslope rain and snow showers to develop over the Laurel \nHighlands by early Saturday morning. Snow accumulations will \ngenerally be less than an inch and limited to the higher \nterrain, though isolated amounts of up to 2 inches will be \npossible by Sunday morning in areas that see the most persistent\nsnow bands.\n\nWinds will be breezy through the weekend with gusts of 20 to 30\nmph possible. The wind, combined with highs only in the 30s and\n40s, will produce wind chill values in the 15 to 30 degree range\nat times. \n\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Dry and seasonal pattern returns next week. \n\nRain and snow showers come to an end by Monday morning as an \nupper level ridge builds into the region. High pressure at the \nsurface and aloft will provide dry conditions with mainly clear \nskies for most of next week along with moderating temperatures.\nHighs should reach the 50s and 60s by Wednesday for all of \nCentral PA.",
